{
	title: 'Restricted subagents: tool list and custom system prompt',
	spec: "SPAWN takes two optional fields that let weak models work without Hal's large prompt. tools: ['read', 'spawn'] restricts the child to these tools; default is all tools. systemPrompt: '...' replaces the WHOLE system prompt (SYSTEM.md, AGENTS.md, skills, user notes); it renders with SYSTEM.md's template language, so variables such as $date and @path imports work, and a $tools line expands to the allowed tools' guide sections plus an Other tools section summarizing the rest of them, rendered from the tool registry (task 5t3). Without $tools the prompt carries no tool guides. The systemPrompt field description (HELP SPAWN) helps the parent write a short prompt and lists the building blocks: $tools, $agents, $skills, $date, $session_cwd. Restrictions bind everywhere a tool can be reached: the provider tool definition, HELP and EVAL's tool(). Host notes that name a disallowed tool (COMPACT, READ_ENTRY) stay: a call to it fails loudly with the available tools, and the case is rare. Any non-empty list brings HELP, which the native tool description names, so a model can refresh a tool syntax; tools: [] sends the provider no tool. A call to a disallowed tool fails as a tool error naming the available tools, and the turn continues. Unknown tool names fail the spawn, listing valid names, so a typo never silently drops a tool. A child's tools are a subset of its parent's; a nested SPAWN cannot widen them, and a child without spawn cannot spawn. mode fork with systemPrompt fails: the copied history assumes Hal's prompt. Restrictions and the custom prompt persist in the session's metadata, survive restarts and /resume, and show in INSPECT; SPAWN's result names them, so the parent knows what the child has. Restriction is a focus aid, not a sandbox: an allowed BASH can do anything.",
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'User, 2026-11-19: weak models are overwhelmed by large system prompts; e.g. a subagent told only "Classify every line of the input and answer in this JSON format" needs none of Hal rules. Antti chose SPAWN { tools?, systemPrompt? } over a profile file with front matter. $tools is the provisional variable name; 10-dad (task 5t3) settles it.',
		'10-dad, 2026-11-19: guides contain directives and vars, which variable values do not re-render, so tools come in through an @tools include line. nativeTool.guides(allowed?) returns the template text; assemble() reads PromptInput.tools (lowercase names, undefined = all), which this task adds.',
		'Implementation: SessionMeta.tools/systemPrompt (set by subagents.spawn, read by promptCache.input); tools.allowed(sessionId) filters tools.all() and tools.run rejects others, which also covers EVAL tool(); HELP lists tools.allowed; round-stream sends no tool def for tools: []; systemPrompt.assemble renders the custom text as <cwd>/<systemPrompt> so @path resolves from the cwd. tools.allowed reads the open session only: tests run tools for sessionless ids, and a session running a turn is always open. Compare tools by name: plugins and tests return fresh tool objects from tools.all().',
		'Checked once with a throwaway host-fixture test (deleted): the child prompt held only the custom text and READ guide; BASH failed naming READ; tools: [] sent no tool def; typo and fork+systemPrompt failed the spawn; SPAWN result named the tools.',
		'Superseded 2026-11-19 (user): the guides are the variable $tools, like $agents, not an @tools include. vars() renders the guide template with the other variables first, since variable values are not re-rendered; prompt-lang lost its generated-include option.'
	],
	needs: ['5t3'],
